[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Both executables are copied into sibling sandbox directories as `driver` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   invoked as `./driver`, so `argv[0]` — which the usage message prints — is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   same string for both programs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Beyond `cargo test`, roughly 25,000 additional argument pairs were compared with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m an out-of-band fuzz harness: the exhaustive cross product of ~60 hand-picked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tokens, random decimal/hex/garbage strings, random 64-bit doubles fed in as C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m hex-float literals, and dense sweeps across the subnormal, overflow and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m underflow boundaries of both `strtod` and `pow`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE`: Rust exited 0 where C is killed by signal 13
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With the read end of stdout (or stderr) closed, the C program dies
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from `SIGPIPE` — shell status 141 — while the Rust program exited 0 and produced
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m no output:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ ./driver 10 308 | true ; echo ${PIPESTATUS[0]}
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 141        # C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0          # Rust, before the fix
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs. A C program inherits the default disposition (`SIG_DFL`), so its first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failing `write` terminates the process. The Rust code additionally discarded the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `io::Error` from `write_all`, so nothing surfaced at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `translation/src/main.rs` calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` as the first statement of `main`, restoring the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disposition. Discarding the write error afterwards is correct: C's `printf`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m likewise ignores a failed write and still `return`s 0/1 (verified by redirecting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to `/dev/full`, where both programs exit 0 for the success path and 1 for the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error path).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Test.** `closed_stdout_pipe_kills_both_programs_with_sigpipe` in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/tests/differential.rs`. It uses `std::io::pipe()` and drops the read
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m end *before* spawning, so the child's first write is guaranteed to hit `EPIPE` —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the test is deterministic rather than racing the child.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that this class of mismatch is invisible to a test that compares
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ExitStatus::code()`, because a signalled process reports `None` on both sides.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `assert_same` therefore compares the whole `ExitStatus`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours that were verified and did *not* mismatch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places a translation of this program is most likely to go wrong.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each was probed specifically; the existing Rust code already matched glibc, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m they are recorded here as verified rather than as defects.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Behaviour | Why it is a trap | Verdict |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Check order per argument: `ERANGE` before `*endptr != '\0'` | `1e999abc` overflows *and* has trailing junk. C reports the range error, not the invalid-input error. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Base validated fully before the exponent is touched | `driver abc def` must print only the base message. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Empty argument accepted as `0` | `strtod("")` performs no conversion, so `endptr == nptr` and `*endptr == '\0'`; the C therefore treats `""` as a valid `0.0`. `driver "" ""` prints `Result: 1.00`. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `endptr` reset to `nptr` when no conversion happens | `" "`, `"+"`, `"--3"`, `"."`, `"0x"`, `"1e"` all leave `endptr` at the *start*, so the echoed string in the error message is the whole argument. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | glibc `strtod` extensions | `inf`/`infinity`/`nan`/`nan(chars)` case-insensitively, C99 hex floats, hex with an optional `p` exponent (`0x10` → 16), `0x` alone consuming just the `0`. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Underflow `ERANGE` = tininess **after rounding** **and** inexactness | `strtod("0x1p-1074")` is an exact subnormal → no `ERANGE`; `strtod("1e-320")` is inexact and tiny → `ERANGE`. Getting this wrong flips exit status on a whole band of inputs. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2.2250738585072012e-308` vs `...13e-308` vs `...14e-308` | Adjacent decimals straddling `DBL_MIN`; only the first sets `ERANGE`. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | glibc `pow` errno rules | `EDOM` only for a negative finite base with a non-integral finite exponent; `ERANGE` for the pole `pow(±0, y<0)`, for overflow, and for underflow **to zero** — a merely subnormal non-zero result sets nothing. No errno at all for NaN operands, `y == 0`, `x == 1`, or any infinite operand. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `pow` underflow boundary | Swept `2^e` for `e ∈ [-1080, -1060]` and `10^e` for `e ∈ [-330, -300]`: `10^-323` is subnormal and quiet, `10^-324` is a range error. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `printf("%.2f")` exactness and tie-breaking | glibc converts the exact binary value and rounds half to even (`0.125` → `0.12`, `0.375` → `0.38`). Verified over `k/2^n` ties and 2,000 random doubles. | matches (Rust's `{:.2}` is exact and rounds half to even) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `%.2f` of huge magnitudes | `10^308` prints all 309 integer digits of the exact double, not `1e308`. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `%.2f` of specials and signed zero | `inf`, `-inf`, `nan`, `-nan` (glibc prints the NaN sign), `-0.00`. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `argv` is bytes, not UTF-8 | Error messages echo the argument verbatim; invalid UTF-8 such as `\xff\xfe` must pass through unchanged. Handled via `OsStrExt`. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Locale | The C never calls `setlocale`, so it stays in the `C` locale and always prints `.` as the decimal point regardless of `LC_NUMERIC`. No divergence to reproduce. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Very long inputs | 100,000-digit integers, 1,000 nines, 300 hex fraction digits, 30-digit exponents — no overflow of the exponent accumulator, no panic. | matches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
